OGUR was a character I started almost 1.5 years ago. I quit playing for 
a while, and recently picked up his game again. I didn't have a log of 
the first part of the game, so details are a little sketchy there.

OGUR was stupid and ugly, the way barbarians are supposed to be. The 
most notable thing in the beginning of the game, was a wand of wishing 
lying around. Being stupid, OGUR wished not for 2 blessed scrolls of 
charging, but for a GDSM. (Note: this might also have been gauntlets of 
power. He had both when I restarted, but the game says I only used one 
wish.) This did help him survive, and stash a lot of stuff in minetown. 
He then forgot about this stuff.

Sokoban was somehow solved (he must be more intelligent than he thinks), 
and yielded a BoH. He also found a lot of shops in the early levels 
(four above level 12).

This was where I stopped playing. When I picked him up again, he was 
somewhere around dungeon level 15, player level 11. He had +5 GoP, a 
BoH, and GDSM. And a 0:0 /oW. Looked promising, so he headed further 
down, since he couldn't enter the quest yet.

He was looting all the vaults he found, when he suddenly felt dizzy and 
materialized outside a large fort. Using the doors well, he killed all 
the soldiers, and then the throne room monsters, and got the loot. He 
started feeling cocky, and taunting the black dragon outside. The black 
dragon responded by disintegrating his +4 GDSM and +1 leather cloak. 
Ouch. His corpse did provide disintegration resistance, though.

The money was worth it, though. OGUR was a follower of Set, but Crom was 
the main god in these dungeons. He had temples in minetown and on three 
dungeon levels. Crom turned into an extortionist of sorts, giving these 
priests his fortune (70K+) for protection, and then killing them, 
getting the money, and moving on to the next priest. This yielded 11 
points of protection (getting it after the initial 9 is *really* hard!), 
and a cloak of magic resistance.

He also converted two of Crom's altars, and summoned Yeenoghu on one by 
offering the priest on it. He sacrificed some more, and got Cleaver. Now 
OGUR had used some different weapons. He had been using a battle-axe for 
a while, and a dwarvish mattock, and now he was two-weaponing a +3 elven 
dagger and a silver saber. This looked better than Cleaver, so he 
stashed it. He sacrificed some more, and one of the monsters he created 
was an Aleax wielding Demonbane. He stashed that, too.

With magic resistance from the cloak, he felt he could go on the quest. 
Coming here, he remembered why he left home to look for the amulet of 
Yendor: it was so boring. Lots of open space, and endless amounts of 
trolls and ogres. One of the trolls happened to be wielding Frost Brand, 
though! He snatched this, and two-weaponed it with his silver saber for 
the rest of the trip. He also found a magic marker here (he already had 
one from the first part of his life).

His fight with Thoth Amon was good. He thought he'd be smart and smash 
him with a potion of paralysis, but this paralysed him as well. Thoth 
Amon pointed this out, and then proceeded to curse OGUR's saber and BoH. 
After uncursing and blessing, OGUR re-evaluated his original plan. "What 
if I resisted something, but he didn't?", he thought. So he wielded a 
potion of sleep, and hit his nemesis with it. OGUR yawned, but Thoth 
Amon fell asleep, and didn't wake up anymore. The Heart of Ahriman was his!

He proceeded down, smashing Medusa and her titan to death (no reflection 
yet). Before heading to the castle, he went back to minetown to donate 
to the priest there (and kill him). Here, he discovered the stash from 
the first part of his adventure, long ago. He had completely forgotten 
about it, but it contained some nice loot: speed boots, a helm of 
telepathy, and some assorted other stuff.

He entered the castle the way a barbarian should: he knocked down the 
front door, filled the moat, and stormed in (after killing everything 
that came out, of course). There was nothing useful in the store rooms. 
He then went down to the fowl smelling place below, and gave all his 
money to the priest there. Then he proceeded down.

None of the demons gave him any trouble, he could kill them in 2-3 hits. 
He found a third magic marker lying around in the mazes (he used ?oMM 
and !oOD to speed up his progress, which worked great). Vlad proved to 
be as wimpy as ever. After finding the vibrating square, he went back up 
for some last preparations.

He still had no reflection, so he reverse genocided silver dragons. None 
of them gave any scales, so he thought he'd try an un-id'ed wand on 
them. It didn't do anything when he engraved with it, but it did bring 
two of the dragons back to life. When he killed the first, it yielded 
scales immediately. He now went to Ludios with a few /oPoly, and made a 
t-shirt (he didn't find any). After enchanting everything up, he had AC 
-43 and two +7 weapons, and went for the final run.

He saw the wizard in his tower, nicely lined up with a titan. One zap of 
a /oD later, both were dead. But before he could even get the amulet, 
the wizard returned, and double-troubled twice. He hacked all three of 
them to death, and wouldn't see the wizard again, except on the plane of 
earth.

When he got down to the vibrating square, he noticed he'd forgotten the 
candelabrum and the bell! So he teleported up, got them, slowly walked 
down again, and performed the ritual. Getting to the sanctum, he wore 
his =oC, zapped a /oD at the high priest, and quaffed a c!oGL to escape. 
Easy.

The way up was easy; the mysterious force didn't bother him much, the 
wizard never appeared, nothing important was cursed, and only one strong 
monster was generated. All the planes were easy as well. On air, he 
didn't get touched by a single air elemental (stealth and invisibility 
is a great combo), and on water, he found the portal immediately when he 
got to the right part of the plane.

The astral plane wasn't as easy. He went right first, toward Famine. 
Famine himself wasn't a problem, but the priests and angels got to him, 
bringing him from ~340hp to ~160. It was Crom's altar, of course, and I 
think OGUR killed the high priest in a fit of rage. After a !oFH, he 
went to the central altar, which was Death's. Death touched him only 
once, but did cost him about 40 hp. The altar was wrong again. On the 
way back he didn't meet him again, and he avoided pestilence completely 
on his way to the last altar.

You offer the Amulet of Yendor to Set... An invisible choir sings, and 
you are bathed in radiance... The voice of Set thunders: 
"Congratulations, mortal!" "In return for thy service, I grant thee the 
gift of Immortality!" You ascend to the status of Demigod...

